    "description": "Count a tab or pane that is gone at the close as closed in launcher stop\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen an orchestrator closes an item, `launcher.py stop` types `/exit` into the implementer session and then closes its tab or pane. Sometimes the tab or pane closes by itself between the check that it exists and the close, and `stop` then exited 6 for a stop that worked. Now `stop` reads the tab or pane again when the close fails: when it is gone, the stop counts as done and exits 0. A failure on a tab or pane that still exists keeps exit 6 and its stderr line.\n\n**Ticket:** N/A\n**Type:** Bug fix\n\n---\n\n## Agent context\n\n### Authority\n\n`skills/orchestrator/references/launcher.md` owns the interface of `launcher.py`, and its \"focus and stop\" section already said that a tab or pane that closed when `claude` ended is not an error.\n\n### What changed, by file\n\n- `skills/orchestrator/scripts/launcher.py`: tmux: when `kill-pane` fails, `stop` checks the pane again, and a pane that is gone is no failure. iTerm2: when the close script (`OSA_STOP`) or the exit script (`OSA_EXIT`) fails, the new `iterm_gone` reads the sessions, and a session that is gone is no failure. A failed read keeps the failure. Herdr: when `hc send /exit` fails, the new `hc_ended` reads `hc get`, and a terminal that is gone or not live is no failure: `stop` goes on and closes the tab. The close of a Herdr tab already read `herdr-app ls` after a failure.\n- `skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-launcher.sh`: new stub switches `tmux-kill-gone`, `osa-stop-gone`, `osa-exit-gone`, `osa-stop-keep` and `hc-send-gone`, and five checks: four gone cases (tmux close, iTerm2 close, iTerm2 exit, Herdr `hc send`) and one iTerm2 close failure on a session that still exists, which exits 6.\n- `skills/orchestrator/references/launcher.md`: \"focus and stop\" says per terminal which failure counts as closed and which exits 6.\n\n### Constraints that applied\n\n- The plugin `version` is not bumped here. The orchestrator owns it and sets it when this PR is next to merge.\n- No `docs/design.md` §15 entry: the orchestrator owns the numbers.\n\n### How this was verified\n\n`bash skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-launcher.sh`: 277 of 277 checks passed at the branch head. Before the fix, the new checks failed (273 of 277).\n\nWhich terminals had the race: tmux had it, as seen once on 0.1.118 (`kill-pane` failed with \"can't find pane\" and exit 6). iTerm2 can have it inside its close script and its exit script. Herdr's close already counted a gone tab as closed, and its shell stays open after `claude` ends, so its tab does not close by itself in normal use. Only `hc send` could fail for a terminal that ended during the send.\n\n### Known gaps\n\nNo live run reproduced the race on any terminal. Only the stubbed test covers the timing.\n\n## Charter\n\nGoal: `launcher.py stop` reports success when the tab or pane closed by itself after `/exit`.\n\nAcceptance criterion: `bash skills/orchestrator/scripts/test-launcher.sh` passes, and holds new checks that `stop` exits 0 with no stderr failure line when the tab or pane is gone at the close step, and that a close failure on a tab or pane that still exists keeps exit 6 and its stderr line.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- Which path does this goal take? The change path (precedent, high).\n- Does the work take the light path? Yes: one package, a runnable criterion, a light band and no open preference (precedent, high).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
